  "delivered_messages_sha256": "1cd6e2312758e84c33547b1fa6114cde94f615a4f8e1ad70665a2fd7817c9f0e",
  "raw_text": "No. A test record without a commit hash or equivalent source identity only proves that *some* code was tested. It cannot reliably establish that the currently checked-out branch—or even its current HEAD—passed.\n\nTo make that claim, the record should tie the result to immutable evidence such as:\n\n- the exact commit SHA,\n- repository identity,\n- clean/dirty working-tree status or a source-tree digest,\n- test configuration and environment,\n- the test result’s timestamp and provenance.\n\nBranch names alone are insufficient because they can move. Without commit identity, rerun the tests on the current checkout.",
